\caption{Repo-level cross-formulation comparison on SWE-bench Verified test set ($n{=}9{,}784$). \cwm{} unparseable outputs default to pass (majority class). Seeds: Qwen3 primary seed 42; DeepSeek \traj{} 6 seeds. \textsuperscript{$\star$}\cwm{} preliminary (training in progress).}
\label{tab:repo_level}
\resizebox{\columnwidth}{!}{%
\setlength{\tabcolsep}{4pt}
\begin{tabular}{lcccc}
\toprule
\textbf{Model} & \textbf{\cls{} Acc (\%)} & \textbf{\cwm{} Acc (\%)} & \textbf{\cwm{} Unparse (\%)} & \textbf{\traj{} Acc (\%)\textsuperscript{$\ddagger$}} \\
\midrule
Qwen3-4B & \textbf{83.63} & 58.04 & 53.70 & TBD \\
Qwen3-8B & 84.48 / 85.60\textsuperscript{$\dagger$} & 65.17 & 73.80 & TBD \\
DeepSeek-6.7B & 74.93 & ${\sim}$60\textsuperscript{$\star$} & TBD & 75.35 $\pm$ 5.00 \\
\midrule
0-shot (Qwen3-8B) & 49.90 & --- & --- & --- \\
0-shot (DeepSeek-6.7B) & 48.50 & --- & 8.70 & --- \\
Majority baseline & \multicolumn{4}{c}{72.96} \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}}

\vspace{2pt}
{\footnotesize \textsuperscript{$\dagger$}Two seeds (42 / 123). \textsuperscript{$\star$}\cwm{} running accuracy; final values pending. \textsuperscript{$\ddagger$}\traj{} reports variant-level accuracy ($n{=}48$); \cls{}/\cwm{} columns report per-test accuracy ($n{=}9{,}784$). \cwm{} parseable-only accuracy: 42.88\% (4B), 38.52\% (8B).}
\end{table}

\paragraph{Formulation divergence.} At repository-level complexity, \cls{} and \cwm{} diverge sharply on Qwen3: the gap is 25.59\pp{} at 4B (\cls{} 83.63\% vs.\ \cwm{} 58.04\%, seed 42; Figure~\ref{fig:scaling_gap}) and 19.31\pp{} at 8B (84.48\% vs.\ 65.17\%). Both gaps are an order of magnitude larger than at function level (1--3\pp{}, Table~\ref{tab:scaling}); formulation effectiveness is complexity-dependent. \cwm{} falls below the majority baseline at both scales ($-$14.92\pp{} at 4B, $-$7.79\pp{} at 8B): negative utility for this model/formulation combination.

\paragraph{Cross-model validation.} DeepSeek-Coder repo-level results (\cls{} final, \cwm{} preliminary) confirm the formulation-inherent nature of the divergence while revealing model-contingent magnitude. Both model families exhibit the same ordering (\cls{} $>$ \cwm{}), but the function$\to$repo degradation differs: Qwen3-8B degrades ${\sim}$5\pp{} for \cls{} and ${\sim}$22\pp{} for \cwm{}, while DeepSeek-6.7B degrades ${\sim}$12\pp{} for \cls{} and ${\sim}$25\pp{} for \cwm{}. The \cwm{} degradation is consistently larger than \cls{} in both families (4--5$\times$ for Qwen3, ${\sim}$2$\times$ for DeepSeek); the generation bottleneck at repo-level complexity is formulation-inherent. The absolute magnitude, however, is model-contingent: DeepSeek shows larger \cls{} degradation (${\sim}$12\pp{} vs.\ ${\sim}$5\pp{}), likely due to model-specific factors such as vocabulary size and tokenizer coverage for repository-level code. The \cwm{} numbers will be updated with final values upon training completion.

\paragraph{Repo-level failure mode taxonomy.} Variant-level analysis ($n{=}48$, Appendix Table~\ref{tab:variant_level}) reveals three distinct failure modes at repo-level complexity. \emph{Format collapse} (\cwm{}): 54--74\% of outputs are unparseable and default to the majority class; variant-level accuracy is ${\sim}$48--52\%. \emph{Decision collapse} (\cls{}, DeepSeek-Coder): per-test accuracy reaches 74.93\%, but fail recall is only ${\sim}$14\%; the model predicts pass on nearly all tests, yielding variant-level accuracy (56.25\%) near the majority baseline (54.17\%). \emph{Aggregation penalty} (\cls{}, Qwen3): per-test accuracy is high (83--86\%), but the all-pass rule amplifies per-test errors; variant-level accuracy ranges from 64.58\% (4B) to 72.92--81.25\% (8B). The same formulation (\cls{}) exhibits qualitatively different failure modes across models: Qwen3 fails at aggregation, DeepSeek at discrimination. \traj{} avoids the aggregation penalty by predicting variant outcomes directly (75.35\% $\pm$ 5.00, DeepSeek-Coder, 6 seeds). No single formulation dominates at repo level; failure mode is a function of formulation $\times$ model $\times$ complexity.

\paragraph{Default assignment artifact.} The apparent \cwm{} ``improvement'' from 4B (58.04\%) to 8B (65.17\%) is an artifact of the default assignment protocol: unparseable outputs receive the majority-class label (pass, 72.96\% of the test set). As unparseable rate \emph{increases} from 53.7\% to 73.8\%, more predictions are replaced by this high-accuracy default, inflating overall accuracy. Parseable-only accuracy \emph{decreases} from 42.88\% (4B) to 38.52\% (8B): the generation model's actual predictive quality degrades with scale at this complexity level.
